use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Filtering import Filtering
from ../../../behaviors/Selection import Selection
from ../../../behaviors/Traversal import Traversal
from ../../../behaviors/Virtualisation import Virtualisation

export ui-component List:
    description:
        A list shows a series of items one after another, usually each on its
        own row. The user can move through it with the keyboard and, if the
        component allows it, pick one or more items.

    behaviors:
        - @{Traversal}
        - @{Selection}
        - @{Virtualisation}
        - @{Filtering}

    items:
        Each item has a name that assistive tools can read out. What else an
        item shows, such as an icon or a second line of text, is up to the
        component using the list.

    picking:
        The component decides whether the list lets the user pick nothing, one
        item, or several. Clicking an item, or pressing the pick key on it,
        picks it. Which key that is is up to the chosen library. The component
        can also have moving to an item with the arrow keys pick it, as
        Selection describes. It is told whenever the picked items change, and
        can change them itself.

    layout:
        Items are usually stacked top to bottom. They can also run across, and
        then Left and Right move between them, reversed for right-to-left
        languages. Or they can wrap into rows and columns, like a set of
        pictures to choose from, and then the arrow keys move in both
        directions as Traversal describes.

    typeAhead:
        Typing letters jumps to the next item whose name starts with what was
        typed, as Traversal describes.

    searching:
        The component can put a search box above the list that narrows the
        items as the user types, as Filtering describes. Typing then goes to
        the box instead of jumping between items.

    groups:
        Items can be put in groups under headings. Headings can't be picked,
        and the arrow keys pass over them.

    disabled:
        Disabled items are shown but can't be picked. The whole list can be
        disabled too.

    empty:
        When there are no items, the component using the list decides what to
        show instead, such as "Nothing here yet".

    long:
        Virtualisation is only switched on for lists long enough to need it.

    nonGoals:
        - No items inside items. That's a tree
        - No column headings. Rows of data under column headings are a table
        - No dragging to reorder
        - Not a form field. What is picked isn't sent with a form; a Select or
            RadioGroup is
